MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Those simulation results make sense.  If the covariates do not have a strong relationship, you shouldn't expect the finite proportions to match the pattern very well.

One thing to remember here is that this is a logistic regression where the response is either 0 or 1, and given imperfect detection you're estimating which value is the true latent state for each site.  It would probably be better to plot the median values for latent occurrence instead of the predicted probabilities.  Then you can see the breakdown of covariate values across sites and which sites were predicted to be occupied on average (see Logistic regression <http://i.stack.imgur.com/7qDiV.png>).  This type of comparison between observed and predicted values will not mirror that of a standard linear regression, and even for a logistic regression it is a bit different because of the imperfect observations.

